                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3c1f4744-947b-376b-abb1-b1eb416463b5",
                        "entry_id": "8c5d412a-c6cd-34e9-9891-a449477db0b6",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Teilgebiet der Mathematik, das die Eigenschaften von Graphen und ihre Beziehungen zueinander untersucht"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2157f02a-c27a-4d50-95dd-cccd1fe7b202",
                                "sense_id": "3c1f4744-947b-376b-abb1-b1eb416463b5",
                                "text": "„Die Aussage min |D| = max |M| ist einer der fundamentalen \"Gleichgewichtssätze\" der Graphentheorie, welcher die beiden Begriffe \"Bedecken\" (= Träger) und \"Packen\" (= Matching) in Beziehung setzt.“"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "23977e8b-18bd-43a1-b0de-60b86754fdcc",
                                "sense_id": "3c1f4744-947b-376b-abb1-b1eb416463b5",
                                "text": "„Ein Bereich, in dem die Graphentheorie erhebliche Beiträge leistet, sei dagegen die Verkehrsplanung. Plant man beispielsweise den Zugverkehr durch Bahnhöfe, dürfen nicht auf einem Gleis zwei Züge in zu engem zeitlichen Abstand einfahren.“"
                            }
                        ],
                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "9685908d-b85d-4956-bb93-cdddb98287a0",
                        "source_entry_id": "8c5d412a-c6cd-34e9-9891-a449477db0b6",
                        "source_sense_id": "3c1f4744-947b-376b-abb1-b1eb416463b5",
                        "target_lang_word": "graph theory",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "graph theory",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f877be4f-b4ed-3f3a-b8b9-f84bf6c4e4ca",
                                        "entry_id": "096d0e18-02d7-3b3f-bbd9-d6b906eec65f",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "The branch of mathematics dealing with the properties of graphs (networks of vertices and edges)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "uncountable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "caee8fbb-517e-4f3d-a8da-9968c0d44cc8",
                                                "sense_id": "f877be4f-b4ed-3f3a-b8b9-f84bf6c4e4ca",
                                                "text": "The type of graph studied in graph theory is formally described as an ordered pair G#61;(V,E) comprising a set V of vertices, or nodes, and a set E of pairs (either ordered or unordered) of vertices, each representing an (directed or undirected) edge; if desired, the vertices and/or edges can be labelled."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4929bb02-e0d4-4e79-837f-3d1779178534",
                                                "sense_id": "f877be4f-b4ed-3f3a-b8b9-f84bf6c4e4ca",
                                                "text": "In graph theory there are two concepts of \"sameness\" whereby graphs are judged to be \"the same\", namely equality and isomorphism.[…]An indication of the pervasive role isomorphism has in graph theory is the fact that isomorphism has virtually captured the word \"is\"."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "2d720617-8443-464d-b4bc-52fc041b0584",
                                                "sense_id": "f877be4f-b4ed-3f3a-b8b9-f84bf6c4e4ca",
                                                "text": "Extremal problems are at the very heart of graph theory. Interpreting it broadly, extremal graph theory encompasses most of graph theory, in its narrow sense, it contains many of the deepest and most beautiful results in graph theory."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7c1243f2-ce49-477a-9c01-b4326e250b60",
                                                "sense_id": "f877be4f-b4ed-3f3a-b8b9-f84bf6c4e4ca",
                                                "text": "We aim to highlight the role of graph theory in modern timetabling research and provide some pointers to the relevant literature for the interested reader."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e61c792b-4040-3426-ba86-4b9a9affcf33",
                                        "entry_id": "096d0e18-02d7-3b3f-bbd9-d6b906eec65f",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A theory developed by applying graph theory to a particular problem or application."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "countable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c6ec3ce8-d86c-4279-973b-7cba89bd47b7",
                                                "sense_id": "e61c792b-4040-3426-ba86-4b9a9affcf33",
                                                "text": "2009, Fu Lee Wang, Christopher C. Yang, Chapter XI: Extracting the Essence: Automatic Text Summarization, Yin-Leng Theng, Schubert Foo, Dion Goh, Jin-Cheon Na (editors), Handbook of Research on Digital Libraries, IGI Global (Information Science Reference), page 114,\nThe entity-level approaches build an internal representation for text units and their relationships, and use graph theories to determine the significance of units."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "8673211a-8e4c-4854-a0f9-1ccd39e924e5",
                                                "sense_id": "e61c792b-4040-3426-ba86-4b9a9affcf33",
                                                "text": "Galloway and Thacker's own dissatisfaction with the graph theories of network science, for example, point to a tendency to attribute unfettered and apolitical naturalness to what are in effect asymmetrical topological spaces."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "450f46a4-57cf-4a61-b66d-b5fa08b3cdbc",
                                                "sense_id": "e61c792b-4040-3426-ba86-4b9a9affcf33",
                                                "text": "Chapter 3 has shown a method that makes use of a graphical formulation employing graph theories for performing the computation of both kinematic quantities (i.e. angular velocities of the center of mass of links, but also linear and angular acceleration), and dynamic (internal forces on the connection elements between the links, but also externally applied wrenches)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
